### Combining Conditions

In Module 03, you learned how `&&`, `||`, and `!` evaluate boolean truth tables. Now we put them to work inside `if` statements to build expressive, robust business rules.

---

### 1. Logical AND (`&&`) — Range & Prerequisite Checks

The `&&` operator requires **every single condition to be true**:

```java
// Checking a valid numeric range [18 to 60]:
if (age >= 18 && age <= 60) {
    System.out.println("Eligible for working age workforce.");
}
```

> [!CAUTION]
> **No Chained Math Syntax in Java**:
> In math, you can write `18 <= age <= 60`.
> In Java, writing `18 <= age <= 60` causes a **compilation error** because `18 <= age` evaluates to `boolean` (`true`), and Java cannot compare `true <= 60`.
> You must write two separate comparisons joined by `&&`: `age >= 18 && age <= 60`.

---

### 2. Logical OR (`||`) — Alternative Qualifiers

The `||` operator requires **at least one condition to be true**:

```java
// Free entry if under 5 years old OR a senior citizen (65+):
if (age < 5 || age >= 65) {
    System.out.println("Eligible for free museum entry!");
}
```

---

### 3. Logical NOT (`!`) — Inverting Decisions

The `!` operator reverses a boolean value:

```java
boolean isLocked = false;

if (!isLocked) { // Reads cleanly: "If NOT locked"
    System.out.println("Door is open. Welcome!");
}
```

---

### Short-Circuit Defensive Guards

Recall from Module 03 that `&&` skips the right-hand side if the left-hand side is `false`. This allows writing **bulletproof defensive guards** on a single line:

```java
// Safe: If str is null, str.length() is NEVER called! Zero NullPointerException!
if (str != null && str.length() > 0) {
    System.out.println("Valid non-empty text: " + str);
}
```

If you used the single `&` operator here instead, Java would evaluate both sides, crashing your application with a `NullPointerException`!

